# clause — Design (top-level binary)

Command-line binary tying together the compiler phase crates
into a single shippable artifact. No algorithmic logic lives
here — `vehje` only parses arguments, reads source files, and
hands inputs to phase crates in order. Every phase owns its
own logic.

The binary is host-side (compiler side), not runtime. It uses
`std` freely. The `no_std` / fixed-arena discipline applied to
`vehje-ir`, `vehje-lex`, `vehje-syntax` does not propagate
upward to this crate.

## Subcommands

Five subcommands name the pipeline stopping point the user
wants. Only `lex` carries real behaviour in the round-one body;
the others reserve their CLI names so the shape is frozen and
reserve their exit-code semantics so scripts against them do
not break as bodies land.

```
clause lex <file>      # tokenise + print tokens
clause parse <file>    # lex + parse + print AST
clause check <file>    # through typecheck + report diagnostics
clause build <file>    # through codegen + emit target
clause run <file>      # compile + invoke interpreter
```

### lex

Reads `<file>` from disk, tokenises it through
`vehje_lex::Lexer::from_str`, and prints one line per token.

Output format:

```
<offset_start>..<offset_end>  <TokenKind>  <byte_literal?>
```

- `offset_start..offset_end` is the half-open byte range in the
  source file.
- `TokenKind` is the `vehje_ir::TokenKind` discriminant.
- `byte_literal?` is the raw source slice for tokens that carry
  user text; empty for structural / trivia-only tokens.

Trivia attached to the next real token prints on a continuation
line with two-space indent.

Exit codes:

- `0` — tokenisation completed without lex diagnostics.
- `1` — at least one lex diagnostic fired (unterminated block
  comment, unclassifiable byte). Diagnostics go to stderr
  prefixed with `file:line:col`.
- `2` — I/O failure (`<file>` missing or unreadable).

### parse / check / build / run

Round-one stubs. Each prints `"<phase> not yet implemented\n"`
to stderr and exits `2`. Their CLI name, argument positional
form, and exit-code envelope are frozen by this DESIGN and do
not change when bodies land.

## Orchestration role

The binary is a dispatch spine. Its files each route one
subcommand:

- `main.rs` — argument parse + subcommand dispatch.
- `args.rs` — hand-rolled CLI parser (no `clap` / `argh` this
  round).
- `lex.rs` — the only real body this round.
- `parse.rs` / `check.rs` / `build.rs` / `run.rs` — stubs.
- `diag.rs` — diagnostic printer (file:line:col prefix plus
  phase tag plus message body).
- `lib.rs` — shared orchestration surface (empty in round one;
  earns content when subcommands share helpers).

Every subcommand file stays under 80 lines. Logic belongs in
the phase crates, not here.

## Dependencies

- `vehje-ir` — shared types (`Diagnostic`, `Span`, `FileId`,
  `TokenKind`).
- `vehje-lex` — the lexer.
- `vehje-syntax` — parse entry.
- `vehje-resolve` — resolve entry.
- `vehje-typecheck` — typecheck entry.
- `vehje-codegen` — emit entry.
- `notko` — `Maybe<T>`, `Outcome<T, E>`.

No `clap` / `argh` dependency. CLI is small enough that a
hand-rolled parser under `args.rs` costs less than the
dependency. Revisit when subcommand count exceeds five or
per-subcommand flag count exceeds three.

## Pass-DAG interaction

`vehje-schedule` is not consumed this round. Round-one
subcommands each correspond to one hard-coded phase chain
(`lex` → just the lexer; `parse` → lex + parse; and so on). A
future round threads the pass-DAG via `vehje-schedule`,
swapping the hard-coded chain for a graph walk.

